# Regular expression in Perl format.
# The pattern is the string it is taken off.
# It starts and ends with slash (e.g. "/(your-expression)/"),
# Also it can be specified by the flag option,
# e.g.
# ```
# (`Q.string.regex "/(word)/i").compiled.matches "WORD"
# ```
#
# There are two entry points and one letter between their names.
# `matches` answers whether the whole text matches: the block it finds has
# to start at zero and end at the length of the text, so a pattern that
# covers only part of it answers `false`.
# `match` searches instead: it hands back a cursor that holds no block of
# its own, whose `next` is the first block found anywhere in the text, and
# the `next` of a block is the one after it.
# ```
# (`Q.string.regex "/[a-z]+/").compiled.matches "1abc"  # false
# ((`Q.string.regex "/[a-z]+/").compiled.match "1abc").next  # block "abc"
# ```
#
# Supported flags:
# /d - Enables Unix lines mode.
# /i - Enables case-insensitive matching.
# /x - Permits whitespace and comments in pattern.
# /m - Enables multiline mode.
# /s - Enables dotall mode.
# /u - Enables Unicode-aware case folding.
#
# The engine is written here, in EO, on top of the byte primitives.
# `compile` reads the expression: the slashes, the body between them and
# the flag letters after the closing one. What comes out is a `pattern`
# holding the body and the flags; `checked` hands the same pattern back
# when the body parses and applies the fallback to the construct the
# parser choked on and to the character offset it sits at otherwise, worded
# as `java.util.regex` words it, so `/a**/` is refused with "Dangling meta
# character '*'" at offset 2 whatever the flags are.
#
# The parser walks the body as bytes, since every metacharacter is ASCII
# and a multi-byte character is copied whole, and turns it once into a tree
# of nodes with the flags baked in. It understands literals, the escapes
# `\\ \/ \. \t \n \r \f \a \e \xHH` and `\Q...\E`, classes with ranges,
# negation and `\d \D \s \S \w \W`, the dot, the anchors `^ $ \A \z \Z \b
# \B`, the quantifiers `* + ? {n} {n,} {n,m}` with a lazy `?` or a
# possessive `+` behind them, alternation, capturing and `(?:)` groups,
# the inline flags `(?idmsux-idmsux)` and `(?flags:...)` scoped the way
# Java scopes them, and `/x` comments. Lookaround, named groups, back
# references, atomic groups, `\uHHHH`, `\p{}`, class intersections and
# the remaining escape letters are refused at compile time with a message
# saying so. A quantifier binds to the last character of a literal run,
# also when that run came out of `\Q...\E`, as in Java.
#
# Every node has a `run` that takes the subject, a byte position, the
# captures found so far and a continuation, and either applies the
# continuation to the position it stops at or answers `nothing`, so
# alternation and quantifiers backtrack by returning to the caller. The
# `/i` flag folds ASCII; with `/u` it also folds Latin-1, Greek and
# Cyrillic letters and treats `i`, `I`, the dotless i and the dotted
# capital I as one letter. `\b` looks at ASCII word characters only.
# Positions in the answer are counted in characters, as before, while the
# engine counts bytes and converts at the border; a matched block remembers
# where its bytes end, so `next` neither rescans the text nor parses the
# pattern again.
#
# @todo #7485:30min Stop skipping 'redundant-attachment' here. It complains
# about three formations, the fallback of `compiled`, the continuation
# `sought` hands to `run` and the one the parser builds for `\z`, and reads
# their `>>` names as unreferenced. At least the one in `sought` does reach
# out of itself, through the receiver its own head declares, and the lint
# misses it, because a void of the same name masks the reference. Dropping
# the names breaks the engine and everything built on it, `string.scanf`,
# `string.as-number` and `path` among them. Remove this directive once
# objectionary/lints#1301 counts a hop through the receiver as reaching
# outward even when the head declares a void of the same name.
#
# @todo #9650:30min Remove `+unlint too-deep-object:1367-1392` from the
#  metas below. It hides how deep `atom` in `pattern` nests its chain of `if.`
#  over the next byte, up to 18 levels where the lint allows 12. Split the
#  chain into objects of its own, so that `atom` nests no deeper.
#
# @todo #9650:30min Remove `+unlint too-deep-object:1744-1755` from the
#  metas below. It hides how deep `refused` in `pattern` nests its chain of
#  `if.` over the escaped byte, up to 15 levels where the lint allows 12. Split
#  the chain into objects of its own, so that `refused` nests no deeper.
#
# @todo #9650:30min Remove `+unlint too-deep-object:1934-1938` from the
#  metas below. It hides how deep `braces` in `pattern` nests the parsing of a
#  counted closure, up to 14 levels where the lint allows 12. Split the parsing
#  into objects of its own, so that `braces` nests no deeper.

